          "Which masters appear in search is determined by your search text (name, @username, speciality or city) and the filters you choose: category, distance, price range, rating, free slots, time of day and style tags. Results are not personalised to your past behaviour. Among the masters that match, those with a rating (at least three reviews) are shown first: the one with the higher average comes first, and with an equal average, the one with more reviews. Masters not yet rated come after them. How a paid Pro or VIP Studio plan changes this order is set out below.",
          "The feed is made of posts from the last 21 days, and their order comes from several things together. The heaviest is how users, including you, responded to a post over the last 7 days: tapping through to book, saving, sharing, commenting, opening it (how long you look at it is not measured) and liking it. The master's rating is added (counted from three reviews upwards), the share of visits the master cancelled themselves among all visits booked with them over the last 180 days is subtracted (counted only when there were at least 5 such bookings; visits cancelled by clients, booking requests the master declined and visits declined or cancelled automatically do not count as cancellations), and a master with free slots sits a little higher; posts from a master with a Pro or VIP Studio subscription get their score raised by 15% (see below). All of it fades with time: a post's weight halves in roughly 36 hours, so a fresh post rises without needing much attention.",
          "So that you see different masters and categories in the feed, one page of the feed (up to 30 posts) shows at most four posts from one master and at most ten from one category – those with the highest score; in addition, the third-newest and older posts of the same master count for less. Posts you have already seen are moved down.",
